@id     05_pointers
@title  Pointers
@topic  pointers
@lang   c
@show   addresses

@theory
A pointer stores the address of another variable.

    int x = 5;
    int *p = &x;   // p points to x
    *p = 10;       // changes x through the pointer, now x == 10

Functions in C get copies of their arguments. To let a function change
the caller's variables, pass their addresses:

    void set_to_zero(int *v) { *v = 0; }
    set_to_zero(&x);

Your task: write swap so that it exchanges the values of two variables.
The program reads a and b and prints them after the swap.

Input:   3 5
Output:  5 3

@read
## Every box has an address
Each variable lives somewhere in memory. That place has a number called its address. `&x` means "the address of x".

## A pointer stores an address
A pointer is a variable that holds the address of another variable. We say it "points to" that variable.

    int x = 3;
    int *p = &x;     // p points to x
    *p = 10;         // change the box p points to: x is now 10

- `int *p` creates a pointer to an int.
- `p = &x` makes p point to x.
- `*p` means "the box p points to". Reading `*p` gives 10; writing `*p = 10` changes x.

## Why pointers are useful
A function gets copies of its arguments. To let a function change the caller's variables, give it their addresses:

    void swap(int *a, int *b) {
        int t = *a;
        *a = *b;
        *b = t;
    }

    swap(&x, &y);    // x and y really change places

## NULL: pointing nowhere
`NULL` means "this pointer has no target". Using `*p` when p is NULL crashes the program (a segmentation fault), so always check before you use it.

## Read more
Beej's Guide to C Programming, chapter 5 "Pointers — Cower In Fear!" — free at beej.us/guide/bgc

@starter
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    // exchange the values that a and b point to
}

int main(void) {
    int x, y;
    scanf("%d %d", &x, &y);
    swap(&x, &y);
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}

@solution
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    int t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}

int main(void) {
    int x, y;
    scanf("%d %d", &x, &y);
    swap(&x, &y);
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}

@test
@input
3 5
@expected
5 3

@test
@input
-1 0
@expected
0 -1

@hidden_test
@input
7 7
@expected
7 7

