#! Steps: Explore -> Understand -> Practice. The task above is the Practice step.
#! ------------------------------------------------------------------

@step explore
@title  Where does the arrow go?
@scene  pointer_basics
@text
Press the buttons and watch where the arrow p points.
p = &a stores the address of a in p. *p = 5 changes the box p points at. Try *p = 5 while p is NULL.

@step watch
@title  Watch swap
@code
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    int t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}

int main(void) {
    int x = 3, y = 5;
    swap(&x, &y);
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}
@text
Press Next line, and on line 11 press Step into to go inside swap.
Watch where a and b point, and how x and y in main change through them.

@step predict
@title  Predict a value
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int x = 3;
    int *p = &x;
    *p = 10;
    printf("%d\n", x);
    return 0;
}
@line   6
@expr   x
@text
What is the value of x after line 6 runs?

@step predict
@title  Predict the target
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int x = 3, y = 7;
    int *p = &x;
    p = &y;
    *p = 1;
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}
@line   6
@expr   p
@text
After line 6 runs, which variable does p point at?

@step predict
@title  Predict the output
@code
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int x = 3, y = 7;
    int *p = &x;
    p = &y;
    *p = 1;
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}
@text
What does this program print?

@step choose
@title  Pick the right line
@code
int x = 3, y = 7;
int *p = &x;
@text
Which line makes p point at y instead of x?
@option p = y;
@option *p = y;
@option p = &y;
@option &p = y;
@correct 3
@answer
&y is the address of y. Storing it in p makes p point at y. *p = y would copy 7 into x.

@step order
@title  Put swap in order
@text
Put the lines of swap in the right order so it exchanges the two values.
@code
void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    int t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}

@step find_bug
@title  Find the bug
@text
Fix swap so that the two numbers really change places.
Right now swap only changes its own copies. Use Debug my code to watch it.
@starter
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int a, int b) {
    int t = a;
    a = b;
    b = t;
}

int main(void) {
    int x, y;
    scanf("%d %d", &x, &y);
    swap(x, y);
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}
@solution
#include <stdio.h>

void swap(int *a, int *b) {
    int t = *a;
    *a = *b;
    *b = t;
}

int main(void) {
    int x, y;
    scanf("%d %d", &x, &y);
    swap(&x, &y);
    printf("%d %d\n", x, y);
    return 0;
}
@test
@input
3 5
@expected
5 3
@hidden_test
@input
-1 8
@expected
8 -1

@step explain
@title  Explain it
@text
Why does swap need pointers? Why doesn't swap(int a, int b) work?
@rubric
copy; address; caller's variables
@answer
C passes arguments by value: swap(int a, int b) gets copies of x and y, so changing
the copies does not change the caller's variables. Passing &x gives swap the address
of x, so *a = ... writes to x itself.

